@meta
id: 06-17
title: Four edits, four different anchors
chapter: 06
type: lesson
skills: find-f, repeat-semicolon, line-end
par: 38
minutes: 6
vscode: four clicks in three lines, plus a tab you must not touch
open: order.resolver.ts errors.json

@brief
A resolver that has been through a bad merge. Four things are wrong, and no
two of them want the same key:

  1. `id:string` has lost the space after its colon.
  2. `viewer` is not always present, so it must become `viewer?: Viewer`.
  3. The return statement is not terminated.
  4. `where:{ id }` has lost its space too.

`errors.json` is open so you can see the codes are already registered. It is
not yours to change — change it and the check fails.

Before you press anything, decide for each edit which anchor is cheapest:
the end of the line, a named character forwards, a named character
backwards, or the repeat of the find you just did.

@keys
| `f:` `F:` | forward or backward to a colon |
| `;` | repeat the last find |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `i` `a` | insert before or after the cursor |

@hints
Edits 1 and 2 are on the same line and both anchored on colons. Do the first
with `f:` and the second with the key that repeats it.
--
Edit 2 wants the `?` before the colon, so insert with `i` there. Edit 1
wants a space after the colon, so insert with `a`.
--
For the last line, terminate it first — that puts you at the right-hand end
— and then travel backwards to the one colon on the line with `F:`.

@solution
    j f : a  <Esc> ; i ? <Esc>
    j A ; <Esc> F : a  <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Nineteen keystrokes for four edits across two lines, and the only thing you
had to think about was which character to name.

The pattern is worth stating plainly, because it is most of what this
chapter is for. In a line of code, every place you want to be is next to a
character you can name — a colon, a comma, a paren, a brace, a quote. So the
question is never how far away is it. The question is which character, which
direction, and on it or beside it. Answer those three and the motion is two
or three keys, every time, regardless of line length.

On the last line, `F:` beats `^` plus `f:` by one key and beats `0` plus a
count by two, because you were already at the right-hand end. Picking the
cheaper direction is a small habit that compounds over thousands of edits.

@start order.resolver.ts
export class OrderResolver {
  public async order(id:string, viewer: Viewer) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne({ where:{ id } })
  }
}

@start errors.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "ORDER_FORBIDDEN": { "statusCode": 403, "sentry": false }
}

@expected order.resolver.ts
export class OrderResolver {
  public async order(id: string, viewer?: Viewer) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne({ where: { id } });
  }
}
